00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:27.740
What do you guys think a win-win-win solution to Oscar and Angela's dispute with the baby poster

543
00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:28.300
would be?

544
00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:34.700
Because, you know, Michael's win-win-win solution is that Oscar will turn the baby poster

545
00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:40.160
into a t-shirt that he has to wear because then he won't see it.

546
00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:41.780
But Angela will see it all the time.

547
00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:43.960
Yes.

548
00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:46.000
And neither of them like that.

549
00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:46.740
They're like, eh.

550
00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:47.660
Neither of them like that.

551
00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:50.220
So what would you make it be, Ange?

552
00:26:50.300 --> 00:26:51.360
What's your win-win-win?

553
00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:54.340
I would say that you could have personal items that could fit on your desk.

554
00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:57.080
And as long as they weren't offensive, they could stay on your desk.

555
00:26:57.080 --> 00:26:57.940
I like that.

556
00:26:57.980 --> 00:27:01.340
You shrink it down to, like, no bigger than an 8x10.

557
00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:03.180
And then she could put it on her desk.

558
00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:04.840
And that would be my win-win-win.

559
00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:08.340
Angela, that is a great solution.

560
00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:10.920
Thank you.

561
00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:15.460
You set a new rule that personal items can be no larger than 8x10.

562
00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:16.680
Just that's it.

563
00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:17.840
That's it, everyone.

564
00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:19.100
You heard it here firSt.

565
00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:20.200
You're welcome, Corporate America.

566
00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:20.920
You heard it here.

567
00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:22.320
All right.

568
00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:23.220
So moving on.

569
00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:24.060
What is next?

570
00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:28.980
So, Angela, we got a really cool fan question about this scene from Maricela Cabuto.

571
00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:34.360
And I loved it so much, I reached out and I asked her if she would send me a recording

572
00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:35.740
of her asking the question.

573
00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:36.480
So here it goes.

574
00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:41.880
When Michael is trying to solve the Angela-Oscar dispute, they are all standing in the accounting

575
00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:42.540
area.

576
00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:48.520
On a column behind Michael, there is a certificate of participation for a Mark Christie.

577
00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:50.080
Who is that?

578
00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:54.500
I was so excited to get this question, Angela.

579
00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:57.980
So this moment happens at 4 minutes, 32 seconds.

580
00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:05.860
And right next to Steve's head on this pillar, reception is behind the pillar, you can see

581
00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:07.040
this plaque.

582
00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:09.040
It's exactly what Maricela described.

583
00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:13.080
It is a certificate of participation for Mark Christie.

584
00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:16.220
This is one of my favorite things about our set.

585
00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:21.180
Okay, so Mark was the best boy grip and the key grip for seasons one through three on the

586
00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:21.540
office.

587
00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:29.540
And it's the art department that is ultimately responsible for any kind of card, sign, certificate,

588
00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:33.120
or logo that's created specifically for the show.

589
00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:35.920
So our art department created the Dunder Mifflin logo.

590
00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:41.880
And they created all of the little plaques and signs and certificates that hang around the

591
00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:42.220
office.

592
00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:49.020
And in an effort to give just like a fun personal touch, they would include the names of real

593
00:28:49.020 --> 00:28:49.680
crew members.

594
00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:50.520
Yes.

595
00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:55.580
So for example, we also had a Salesman of the Year and a Humanitarian of the Year plaque.

596
00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:59.160
And those plaques featured our crew member names as well.

597
00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:01.340
But really, you can see them everywhere.

598
00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:03.500
You'll start to notice them as you watch episodes.

599
00:29:03.640 --> 00:29:04.240
I do now.

600
00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:05.200
I look for them.

601
00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:05.480
Yeah.

602
00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:07.980
There are tons that are just sprinkled throughout the whole office.

603
00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:09.840
Well, let me tell you what my favorite one is.

604
00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:10.240
Okay.

605
00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:10.640
Okay.

606
00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:16.280
So my favorite one involves our first AD, Kelly Cantley.

607
00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:17.820
You guys might remember her.

608
00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:21.680
Kelly, her job is keeping all of the actors on schedule.

609
00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:25.520
She makes sure that we arrive for our scenes when we're supposed to.

610
00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:28.960
And she is also the one who would run our safety meeting.

611
00:29:29.180 --> 00:29:29.420
Yes.

612
00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:34.140
So Kelly Cantley was safety meeting, lit candle on set.

613
00:29:34.340 --> 00:29:35.360
That's Kelly Cantley.

614
00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:38.620
We have a pair of scissors in someone's hand.

615
00:29:38.920 --> 00:29:39.660
Safety meeting.

616
00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:40.580
Yes, exactly.

617
00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:41.120
Exactly.

618
00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:45.560
But Kelly really had to keep the whole production on time.

619
00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:51.600
Everything happening on the set, all of us working together and getting our day done,

620
00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:52.480
making our day.

621
00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:58.480
She's by far one of the most important elements to running the show, for sure.

622
00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:06.900
Well, I guess that Kelly had gone around and looked at all the plaques and she noticed that

623
00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:08.860
her name was not on any of them.

624
00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:12.740
I can kind of picture Kelly doing that, actually.

625
00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:19.040
She spent a couple of days sort of doing this bit where she was all like, oh, I see.

626
00:30:19.900 --> 00:30:21.460
No, no, Kelly Cantley.

627
00:30:21.580 --> 00:30:22.760
There's my name is on any plaque.

628
00:30:22.780 --> 00:30:23.660
It was very funny.

629
00:30:23.660 --> 00:30:29.000
And then all of a sudden, one day, a new plaque showed up.

630
00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:32.980
It's on the pole over by Stanley's desk.

631
00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:34.600
And here's what it said.

632
00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:43.780
It said, whereas it came to our attention that Kelly Cantley was whining, that a lowly

633
00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:46.940
second assistant director had a plaque with her name on it.

634
00:30:46.940 --> 00:30:53.180
And whereas Kelly Cantley made a comment on the air regarding that fact, we have decided

635
00:30:53.180 --> 00:30:56.840
to give her this certificate because we love her.

636
00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:00.020
Isn't that so fun?

637
00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:03.560
And so and then they just waited for her to notice it.

638
00:31:03.640 --> 00:31:06.220
And then it stayed there for the rest of the run of the show.

639
00:31:06.380 --> 00:31:08.840
So, guys, something fun to do.

640
00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:15.140
You can go through, try to hit pause whenever you see these certificates and you can see some

641
00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:16.540
names of our crew members.

642
00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:19.300
I think it's just a really, really great way to honor them.

643
00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:23.420
And this is actually a very popular thing that happens on sets.

644
00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:24.320
Yes.

645
00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:25.000
All right.

646
00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:25.980
I love that question.

647
00:31:25.980 --> 00:31:29.040
I love hearing the behind the scenes of our crew.

648
00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:35.060
But Jenna, it's time to get into the save the date.

649
00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:35.860
Yes.

650
00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:37.780
Six minutes, 28 seconds.

651
00:31:37.780 --> 00:31:40.200
We get a real bird's eye view of this.

652
00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:46.300
Well, Pam has broken down and is giving Angela a save the date.

653
00:31:46.300 --> 00:31:47.220
Hand-delivered.

654
00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:50.100
We have a fan question from Miss Lavender Darling.

655
00:31:50.660 --> 00:31:55.440
She said, Pam's save the date invitations say place TBA.

656
00:31:56.740 --> 00:32:02.460
Why did she bother making a save the date with a location TB?

657
00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:03.460
TBD.

658
00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:05.220
When I looked at the save the date.

659
00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:07.140
OK, first of all, Angela's throwing shade.

660
00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:08.920
She's like, it's hand-delivered, but it's OK.

661
00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:10.340
But it's also not my taste.

662
00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:12.420
So she's throwing a lot of shade.

663
00:32:12.580 --> 00:32:14.980
But I screen-grabbed the save the date.

664
00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:17.040
And it says, save the date.

665
00:32:17.300 --> 00:32:19.600
And then written in hand.

666
00:32:19.760 --> 00:32:22.800
So it's printed, but then you've got to fill in everything by hand.

667
00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:26.140
It says, for the wedding, Pam Beasley to Roy Anderson.

668
00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:27.200
Date June 10th.

669
00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:28.640
Place to be determined.

670
00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:31.620
The save the date gives us nothing.

671
00:32:32.600 --> 00:32:38.920
Everyone in the office knew it was going to be June 10th because Roy announced it drunkenly

672
00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:39.840
on Booze Cruise.

673
00:32:40.560 --> 00:32:42.620
We know it's between the two of you.

674
00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:44.760
This save the date is pointless.

675
00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:45.500
Yeah.

676
00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:46.720
It's a waste of time.

677
00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:47.940
It's a waste of money.

678
00:32:48.220 --> 00:32:49.120
Kind of like their wedding.

679
00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:50.540
Oh, my God.

680
00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:52.380
Can I get an Angela Sass, please?

681
00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:52.780
I didn't.

682
00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:53.480
I did.

683
00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:53.980
Sam.

684
00:32:54.240 --> 00:32:55.300
Angela Sass.

685
00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:56.200
There it is.

686
00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:57.820
I agree with you, Angela.

687
00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:06.020
I think that these rather lame, handwritten, save the dates are a waste of time.

688
00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:08.980
Did you do save the dates for your wedding?

689
00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:10.540
Or did you just send an invitation?

690
00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:14.720
Well, you know, I did just an email save the date.

691
00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:15.380
Yeah.

692
00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:16.680
I did an email.

693
00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:16.900
Yeah.

694
00:33:17.020 --> 00:33:18.600
That's a little bit where we are now.

695
00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:23.600
I think I'll say I did a save the date.

696
00:33:23.600 --> 00:33:26.300
A hard copy, save the date thing, I think.

697
00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:27.600
Why?

698
00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:29.280
It's a waste of time.

699
00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:29.980
I don't know.

700
00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:31.680
It's a waste of stamps.

701
00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:33.260
It's because it was like etiquette back then.

702
00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:33.960
Back then.

703
00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:36.720
Way back then.

704
00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:38.320
No, but it's true.

705
00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:40.200
I think there's a lot that has changed.

706
00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:46.540
I remember getting in a huge argument with my mom about wedding planning.

707
00:33:46.540 --> 00:33:54.040
Because in her generation, the bride planned the wedding with her parents because her parents

708
00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:55.060
paid for the wedding.

709
00:33:55.240 --> 00:34:00.160
And it was like a party they threw for the bride and groom.

710
00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:06.520
And I said, I want to plan my wedding with my husband, the person who's going to be my

711
00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:06.960
husband.

712
00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:14.480
Like, my mom wanted to pick out our registry items with me.

713
00:34:14.660 --> 00:34:20.900
And I was like, that feels so weird to me that I would go with my mom to pick out my

714
00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:21.320
dishes.

715
00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:26.560
Wouldn't I go with the person who's going to eat off the plates with me to pick them out?

716
00:34:26.720 --> 00:34:31.520
But there was like a generational divide in like how these things are done.

717
00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:35.760
And I feel like this is going to point to like another generational divide.

718
00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:41.380
Like, remember when you used to send these paper save the date or even these paper wedding

719
00:34:41.380 --> 00:34:41.960
invitations?

720
00:34:42.740 --> 00:34:47.280
Nowadays, I get invited to weddings all the time where it's all online.

721
00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:53.400
I get an email and then I click on this website and the registry links are there and the RSVP

722
00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:54.000
is there.

723
00:34:54.160 --> 00:34:56.760
And it's like all there and it's actually kind of awesome.

724
00:34:57.020 --> 00:34:59.200
I like a lot of it that's online now.

725
00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:05.700
It makes it very easy to find information, but I still like getting a hand proper invitation.

726
00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:07.380
I don't need to save the date.

727
00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:09.800
I don't need a little card with directions.

728
00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:14.200
I don't need a little special card that lets you know if I want to eat chicken or the

729
00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:18.460
pasta, but I like getting the paper invitation.

730
00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:21.320
For some of my dearest friends, I've saved them.

731
00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:21.860
I don't know.

732
00:35:21.980 --> 00:35:23.820
I also am a little bit of a pack rat, as we know.

733
00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:24.720
I scrapbook.

734
00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:28.780
So I like getting the actual paper invitation myself.

735
00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:30.260
I can be with you on that.

736
00:35:30.520 --> 00:35:34.960
I really like getting a paper thank you.

737
00:35:35.540 --> 00:35:36.000
Yeah.

738
00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:38.740
Like a thank you card in the mail.

739
00:35:38.840 --> 00:35:39.140
That's nice.

740
00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:43.120
As opposed to for a big event like a wedding.

741
00:35:43.340 --> 00:35:46.540
I don't need like a paper thank you if I gave you a birthday present or something.

742
00:35:46.540 --> 00:35:51.880
But if it's a big event like a wedding, I think the process of a couple sitting down and writing

743
00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:55.260
out their thank you cards is a really nice tradition.

744
00:35:56.080 --> 00:35:59.400
And I think it is.

745
00:35:59.620 --> 00:36:00.080
Hmm.

746
00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:01.280
What's the word I'm looking for?

747
00:36:01.640 --> 00:36:02.120
Proper.

748
00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:08.040
I feel like if you're giving like, yeah, like the significance of a wedding gift deserves

749
00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:10.320
the significance of a handwritten thank you card.

750
00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:12.140
I can go with that.

751
00:36:12.260 --> 00:36:13.040
I can go with that.

752
00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:18.160
Well, Angela, I have to say that for being an episode about conflict resolution, we just

753
00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:19.820
did not have a conflict about that.

754
00:36:19.980 --> 00:36:20.800
We were in total agreement.

755
00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:23.200
